A||||7||Cheekisy||HELP!!! major problem with my fiero||||1||
Z||000000||Cheekisy||05-09-2001||04:27 PM||||  I have a '86 SE 4 cyl with an auto trans.  The problem is that it wont shift into Park, so i cant take my key out and since my e-brake isnt working right now i have to park on perfectly flat surfaces.  My uncle said it may just be the cable going from the transmission to the shifter.  Please give me any info u have on this problem it is very annoying.  Also, if i actually need it, how much does a new auto trans cost?||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||05-09-2001||04:39 PM||||theres an interlock on trans shifter that allows the ignition key to be turned off and removed. Im not sure about Fiero, some are a manual type linkage and some are electric solenoid. I think thats where your problem is - not in the trans. It should be under the console on the actual shifter, or running up the steering column. A new trans costs more than finding another Fiero. If you do need one ever, best bet is finding a good used one or a whole complete parts car with a good one.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fformula88||05-09-2001||04:47 PM||||I dropped a screw down one of the openings for the ashtray once.  A few days later I couldn't shift into park!  It had gotten itself under the shifting mechanism and would allow it to go into any gear except park!  If you take the center console apart to check the linkage, be sure to check for coins, screws etc interfering with it too!||||reg||1
Z||000003||thomas_l||05-09-2001||04:52 PM||||Funny you mention stuff falling down in the console... A few weeks back I was hunting for misc parts in the junkyard and decided to harvest the 4-sp shifter out of one (my junkyard mail-order tranny turned out to be a 4 sp not 5 as advertised - but that's another long story  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">). When I pulled out the shifter I ended up with a handfull of coins, mostly quarters. Not a bad deal - shifter for $15, less $10 in change I netted out at $5.<P>Now if I could just find one with hundreds stuffed under the shifter...||||reg||10
Z||000004||Wolfhound||05-09-2001||04:58 PM||||Does the park lockout button feel any different when you press it (Harder or easier)? Remove the shift knob by pulling out the clip on the radio side and make sure the nylon piece inside is OK. If it is remove the boot and replace the knob. See if the action continues through the shift lever.<P>If all this works it may be the interlock cable that runs forward to the switch on the column.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wolfhound||05-09-2001||05:07 PM||||Is this what your talking about Thomas?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/money.JPG">  average Fiero is good for about 5 bucks of dirty money.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||stsmithgt||05-09-2001||06:16 PM||||Eeeeeeewwwww.... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I use the door pocket for a change holder so I only chased down a few gum wrappers and dust. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||05-09-2001||08:31 PM||||Notes about the transmission interlock are in my cave. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Ogre's Fiero Cave</A> steering column article in the interior section. <P>the tranny cable is adjusted by releasing a clip on the bracket on the tranny. You CAREFULLY pull/pry up the clip. Put the shifter in park. (Make sure the tranny stays in park...) then put the clip back in. (The clip doesn't come all the way off.)<P>All this of course is AFTER you clean out the crap under the console.....<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-09-2001).]||||reg||1
